452. Design Selenium framework for e-commerce.
⭐ Interview Answer

“For an e-commerce application, I would identify major business modules such as login, product search, product details, cart, checkout, payment and order history.

I would create separate Page Objects for each module and keep business workflows separate from low-level Selenium operations.

For example, the checkout test should call methods such as addProductToCart() and completeCheckout() rather than directly containing locators and Selenium commands.

I would also support different test data such as products, users, addresses and payment scenarios through externalized test data.

Smoke, regression and integration tests would be organized using TestNG groups. The framework would run in parallel through Jenkins and generate reports and screenshots for failures.”

Example:

Tests
 ├── LoginTests
 ├── SearchTests
 ├── CartTests
 ├── CheckoutTests
 └── OrderTests

Pages
 ├── LoginPage
 ├── ProductPage
 ├── CartPage
 ├── CheckoutPage
 └── OrderPage
453. Design framework for healthcare.
⭐ Interview Answer

“For healthcare, I would focus not only on UI automation but also on API and database validation because many critical workflows involve backend processing.

For example, patient registration could be validated through UI, the corresponding API response could be validated through API automation, and the patient record could be verified in the database.

I would also make test data isolated because healthcare applications contain sensitive information. Real patient information should never be used in automation.

I would implement role-based test scenarios for users such as doctors, nurses, administrators and patients, depending on the application.”

