🔥 Never do
driver.get("https://qa.example.com");

inside every test.

458. How would you support Chrome/Firefox/Edge?
⭐ Interview Answer

“I would create a DriverFactory and make the browser configurable through a system property. I would avoid creating WebDriver instances directly inside test classes.”

Example:

String browser = System.getProperty("browser", "chrome");

switch(browser.toLowerCase()) {

    case "chrome":
        driver = new ChromeDriver();
        break;

    case "firefox":
        driver = new FirefoxDriver();
        break;

    case "edge":
        driver = new EdgeDriver();
        break;

    default:
        throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid browser");
}

Execution:

mvn test -Dbrowser=chrome

or:

mvn test -Dbrowser=firefox
459. How would you support parallel execution?
⭐ Interview Answer

“I would use TestNG parallel execution and make WebDriver thread-safe. The most important part is that different threads must not share the same WebDriver instance.”

Example:

<suite name="Regression" parallel="tests" thread-count="4">

For thread safety:

private static ThreadLocal<WebDriver> driver =
        new ThreadLocal<>();

Then:

driver.set(new ChromeDriver());

Each thread gets its own driver.

🔥 Client may ask:

Why ThreadLocal?

Answer:

“Because if multiple tests run simultaneously, sharing one WebDriver can cause tests to interfere with each other. ThreadLocal maintains a separate WebDriver instance per thread.”

