467. How would you handle test data?
⭐ Interview Answer

“I would keep test data separate from test logic. Depending on the application, test data could come from JSON, Excel, CSV, properties files, databases or API-generated data.

For parallel execution, I would ensure that tests don't modify the same data simultaneously.”

Example:

testdata/
 ├── login.json
 ├── users.json
 ├── products.json
 └── checkout.json
Better approach

For dynamic applications:

API → Create test data
        ↓
UI → Execute test
        ↓
Cleanup

This is often better than maintaining thousands of static records.

468. How would you handle secrets?
⭐ Very important

“I would never hard-code passwords, API keys or tokens in source code or Git.

I would store secrets in Jenkins Credentials, environment variables or a secure secrets-management solution and inject them during execution.”

❌ Don't:

String password = "MyPassword123";

Better:

String password = System.getenv("PASSWORD");

Or retrieve it securely through CI/CD secret management.

🔥 Client-round statement

“Configuration can be committed; secrets should not be committed.”

469. How would you reduce execution time?
⭐ Strong answer

“I would first identify the execution bottlenecks and then optimize them.”

My approach:

1. Parallel execution
500 tests
   ↓
10 parallel workers
2. Avoid unnecessary waits

Don't:

Thread.sleep(5000);

Use:

WebDriverWait
3. Run only required suites
Smoke → quick feedback
Regression → full validation
4. API instead of UI where appropriate

Instead of:

UI → Create user
UI → Configure user
UI → Execute test

use:

API → Create user
UI → Execute actual UI scenario
5. Reduce repeated login

Use appropriate authentication/session strategies.

6. Parallelize independent tests
470. How would you make framework maintainable?
⭐ This is one of the most important questions.

“I would focus on separation of concerns, reusability, loose coupling and clean coding standards.

Test cases should contain business scenarios, page classes should contain UI interactions, utilities should contain reusable functions, and configuration should be externalized.

I would use stable locators, reusable components, centralized driver management, centralized waits, proper logging and reporting.

I would also enforce code reviews and coding standards and continuously refactor duplicate code.

The framework should be easy for a new team member to understand and extend without modifying existing tests unnecessarily.”

